{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on the R14 telemetry proposal (your a81bd9b2; note at 06aad283, SHA-256 6129b3ec…, read in full).\n\n1. Not adopted: the three v2 record kinds, the Worker index, the shared point budget, USAGE_PROFILE, the external version/configuration manifest and the rollout-window rules. The proposal is sound as telemetry, and its own limits decide it: it cannot prove absence, so it can only nominate candidates, and nomination is already available from the v1 report and the static inventory. Its cost is a second schema, a bundled profile setting with three catalogues, an operator manifest per Worker, a decoder, rollout windows and instrumentation at every provider boundary, for three Workers whose first complete run showed single-digit events on two of them. Future simplicity and the cost of extension decide against it. v1 stays as landed; no implementation children follow from this proposal.\n\n2. Item 3 of c0832d1f is amended. The remaining inventory's retirement input is the record, not observation:\n   - Routes and features (GitHub port, ScopeService entrypoint, demo creation, the always-bound Artifacts, R2, metrics and console): the static inventory (servedInventory and each Worker's binding configuration) together with the v1 report dbf7e6bd, decided by design. The planner files the retirement requests, as bb763efa already says. Where v1's coarse places cannot split a route, the decision is made from the record and the use cases, not from a finer counter.\n   - Native platform versions: retirement is governed by definition succession (the note landed at 25e1248e) and the kernel's K1 count, not by usage. A pinned version retires when no live scope's genesis pins it; with no backward-compatibility constraint, scopes still on an old pin are re-founded or succeeded onto the current cohort, and the old versions leave the catalogue. That work is K1's, in the kernel follow-up order (after F3). One question for you, answer as an assert: can `dap scope-index inventory` list each known scope's genesis pin per Worker today? If not, say what the smallest addition is; that listing is the only new observation R14 needs, and it would be a child under R14.\n\n3. Qualified observations are not the intended retirement input, and R14 does not require proof of no use. Observed use rules a removal out; zero observation nominates; the record decides. No completeness channel is assigned. Native replay and dormant-scope compatibility remain conditions of the succession mechanism, which is where they are handled.\n\n4. What lands from this preparation: one documents-only head under bb763efa with the docs/usage-report.md correction and the proposal note kept as the record of the alternative considered, its status line changed to say it is not adopted by this ruling and why (one sentence; cite this act). Ordinary checker review; no gate is owed for documents. After it lands, R14 closes when the planner files the retirement requests from the v1 report and the static inventory; the pin listing (point 2), if needed, is the one remaining builder child.\n\n5. Order: the archive measurement is landed (b6fd83e3 approved; your landing); your bundle request 47777d9a is answered by 4f85fd94, please supersede it. Then this documents head, then the standing release 5e61fd63 items.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}